; The canonical change from S6 to S7.
;
; S6 |- S7. Strata can reach the host, and only where it says so.
;
; Strata has no primitives. Data, definitions, application, `let` and `match`,
; and nothing else -- which is what makes a compiler for it writable in it,
; and also what stops it comparing a character or hashing a byte. Every layer
; above this one needs at least one of those.
;
; So the boundary is drawn rather than the primitives smuggled in. A primitive
; is declared: a name, a type, and the capability it needs, with no body. What
; it does is the host's, and three things must hold for that to be worth
; anything. The set the module declares is the set the host provides, name for
; name and arity for arity. A value that crosses out and back is the value
; that set out. And using a primitive needs its capability -- which is S6's
; rule, unchanged, because a primitive declares `uses` like anything else.
;
; Only the first two are new. The third costs nothing, which is the argument
; for having built S6 before this.
(change
  (kind foundation)
  (name "S6->S7")
  (predecessor (foundation-of "foundations/S6"))
  (delta-meta (file "changes/S6-S7/delta-meta.canon"))
  (delta-grammar (file "changes/S6-S7/delta-grammar.canon"))
  (feature
    (feature
      (name "the native boundary")
      (programs [
        (file "features/strata/native.generated.meta")
      ])
      (sources [
        (text-file "features/strata/native.meta")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.grammar")
        (text-file "languages/strata/strata.meta")
        (text-file "strata/system/native.strata")
      ]))))
